# INFIX-TO-POSTFIX-CONVERSION-USING-REVERSE-POLISH-NOTATION
This C project converts infix expressions (like A+B) to postfix notation (like AB+) using a LIFO stack,
handling operator precedence and associativity across modular files (header.h, main.c, pushpop.c, precedence.c, convert.c) built with a Makefile.


Concepts Used
1.	Infix notation
The operator is written between operands. Example: A+B.
2.	Postfix notation / RPN
The operator is written after the operands. Example: A+B becomes AB+.
3.	Stack (LIFO)
A stack follows Last-In, First-Out. push() inserts an item and pop() removes the top item.
4.	Operator precedence and associativity Operator Priority Associativity
^	3 (highest) Right to left
* and /	2	Left to right
+ and -	1 (lowest) Left to right
5.	Modular programming and Makefile
A large program is split into several .c files, each doing one job. A shared header file holds common
declarations. A Makefile tells the make utility how to compile each file into an object file and link them into one executable.


